<section id="A5.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.1.4 </span>Reverse Stitching</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In P-GRAFT, we always use the fine-tuned model for the first <math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-N_{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-N_{I})</annotation></semantics></math> steps and then switch to the reference model. We experiment with a reverse stitching strategy, where we use the reference model for the earlier denoising steps and fine-tuned model for the later denoising steps. For switching timestep <math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote this strategy as RP-GRAFT (<math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{+}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{+}I</annotation></semantics></math>) - i.e. RP-GRAFT (<math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75N</annotation></semantics></math>) indicates that the base model will be used from <math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0.75N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0.75N}</annotation></semantics></math>, after which the fine-tuned model will be used. From Table <a href="#A5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ E.1.4 Reverse Stitching ‣ E.1 Ablations ‣ Appendix E Text-to-Image Generation ‣ Fine-Tuning Diffusion Models via Intermediate Distribution Shaping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, we observe that this strategy is significantly worse when compared to P-GRAFT - this provides further evidence of the bias-variance tradeoff. Ablations are done with <math id="A5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=100,K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=100,K=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Ablations on reverse stitching</figcaption>
<div id="A5.T12.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:192.4pt;height:60.8pt;vertical-align:-28.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-19.7pt,6.2pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
